# Frontend copy — user instructions only

Visible Console copy is an instruction or fact for the human. Never render a
system instruction: a prompt, constraint, or procedure written for a coding
agent, LLM, dataset agent, MCP tool, or the product itself.

Same bar for backend strings the Console displays (errors, toasts, empty
states). British English, sentence case, acronyms preserved — `DESIGN.md`.

## Audience test

Before a string is rendered (heading, description, empty state, button, toast,
tooltip, helper, aria-label, placeholder):

- Would a **user** act on this? Keep.
- Would an **agent or model** act on this? Do not render. Put it in a
  copy-paste payload, `chat.mutate(...)`, or an API body.

Instrument voice: state the fact and stop. No rationale, reassurance, or
coaching ("9 rows", never "9 rows — small enough to read").

## Payloads vs display

Clipboard prompts (`onboardWithAiBootstrapPrompt`, `telemetrySetupPrompt`),
`chat.mutate` strings, and skill/MCP text are **payloads**. They may contain
system instructions. Surrounding UI only says a prompt exists and what the
user should do with it.

A capability's own system prompt is **user data**. Show it in a prompt viewer.
Do not recast it as Console guidance.

## Examples

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — agent procedure as empty-state copy
description="The agent runs init sync first; capability scan follows in the skill."

// ✅ GOOD — what the user does, then the fact
description="Paste the onboarding prompt in your coding agent. A project appears after overmind sync."
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — bootstrap prompt rendered on screen
<p>Use the published PyPI package only — do not install from git.</p>
<p>Reload the IDE once after sync; do not re-export the key.</p>

// ✅ GOOD — user instruction; payload stays on the clipboard
<Button>Copy onboarding prompt for Cursor</Button>
<p>Open the repository in Cursor, then paste the prompt.</p>
```

Do not use "You are…", "Do not…", "Never…", "Always…" aimed at an agent as UI
copy. Do not paste a skill body, clipboard payload, or chat-agent instruction
into a label or empty state.
